# Event Card Thumbnail Images — Size & Layout Rules

These rules govern every image used inside an `.event-item` card (Upcoming **and**
Past events) in `index.html` and `events.html`. Following them means a new poster
"just works" as a thumbnail with no per-card CSS overrides.

## 1. How the thumbnail is displayed (do not fight this)

The image lives in `.event-image > img` and is rendered with **`object-fit: cover`**.
`cover` **fills the box and crops whatever doesn't fit** — it never letterboxes and
never distorts. The visible box is **not** a fixed shape; it changes per breakpoint:

| Breakpoint | `.event-image` box (W × H) | Box ratio | What `cover` crops |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Desktop ≥ 769px | **150px** × card height (~180–280px) | ~0.55–0.83 (portrait) | a little off top/bottom |
| events.html ≤ 768px | **90–110px** × card height | portrait | a little off top/bottom |
| index.html ≤ 768px (stacked) | **full width (~345px)** × **180px** | ~1.9 (landscape) | **a lot off top & bottom** |

> Because the same image is shown as a tall portrait on desktop **and** a short
> landscape band on mobile, the only region guaranteed to always be visible is the
> **center**. Design for the center.

## 2. Required source-image specs

- **Aspect ratio: 2:3 portrait (0.667).** This matches the reference past poster
  `images/spark_kerala_event_2026_1.jpg` (1024 × 1536) and crops cleanly at every
  breakpoint. Do not submit landscape or square posters as the card image — resize/
  recompose them to 2:3 first (this is exactly the fix that made Spark & Veena work).
- **Dimensions: 1000 × 1500 px** recommended (min **800 × 1200**, max **1200 × 1800**).
  This is ~2× the largest display size (≈345px) so it stays crisp on retina screens.
- **Format:** `.jpg` (quality ~80%) for photographic posters; `.png` only when the
  art needs hard-edged text/logos or transparency. Use `.webp` if a matching
  fallback exists.
- **File size:** keep **under ~300 KB**. Compress before committing.
- **Color space:** sRGB.

## 3. Safe-area / layout rules (what goes *inside* the image)

`cover` crops the edges, and crops the **top & bottom hardest** (mobile landscape
band). So:

- Keep all **critical content** — event title, headliner faces, date, sponsor
  logos, contact info — inside the **central safe zone**:
  - vertically: the **middle 60%** (leave the top 20% and bottom 20% expendable),
  - horizontally: the **middle 90%**.
- Put the **main subject / title in the dead center**, not near any edge.
- Let the background **bleed to all four edges**; never rely on a border, frame, or
  edge text being visible.
- Avoid tiny print near edges (phone numbers, fine print) — it will be cropped on
  mobile. Put that info in the card **text**, not baked into the image (see §5).

## 4. CSS rules — keep parity with past events (no per-card overrides)

- The card uses the shared rules only:
  `.event-image { width:150px; flex-shrink:0 }` and
  `.event-image img { width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover }`.
- **Do NOT** add `.event-item-<name>` overrides such as `object-fit: contain`,
  a grey/letterbox `background`, `align-self: stretch`, flex centering, or an
  explicit `border-radius` on the image. They were removed on purpose — a correctly
  sized 2:3 image needs none of them.
- **Rounded corners are automatic.** They come from the card itself
  (`.event-item { overflow:hidden; border-radius:10px }`) and only show when the
  image *fills* the column — which `cover` + a 2:3 image guarantees.

## 5. Card markup & text rules (controls thumbnail height)

The card height is driven by the **text column**, and the image stretches to match
it. Long text → tall card → the image is cropped harder. Keep upcoming-event cards
as short as the past ones:

- Mirror the past-event structure exactly:
  ```html
  <div class="event-item">
    <div class="event-image"><img src="images/<file>" alt="<event name>"></div>
    <div class="event-content">
      <h5 class="event-title">Title</h5>
      <div class="event-address">One short description line</div>
    </div>
    <div class="event-date">
      <div class="event-time">YYYY</div>
      <div>MON-MON</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  ```
- Keep `.event-content` to a **title + ~2 lines** max. Do not embed full performer
  lists, sponsor blocks, or booking details in the card — link out or use a details
  page. (Aim for a card height of ~180–280px, matching Past Events.)

## 6. File naming

Use lowercase, hyphen/underscore, descriptive, dated:
`<event-slug>_<year>[_<season>]_thumb.<ext>` — e.g. `spark_kerala_2026_aug_thumb.jpg`.
Always set a meaningful `alt` describing the event.

## 7. Pre-commit checklist

- [ ] Image is **2:3 portrait** (≈1000×1500), sRGB, < ~300 KB.
- [ ] Title + main subject are **centered**; nothing critical in the top/bottom 20%.
- [ ] Card uses the shared markup; **no per-card image CSS overrides** added.
- [ ] `.event-content` is title + ≤2 lines (card stays short like Past Events).
- [ ] `alt` text set; filename follows the naming convention.
- [ ] Verified at desktop **and** mobile widths that nothing important is cropped.
